\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Sample-size sweep on Moirai-Small/ETTh2} ($h{=}96$, condition~B). On held-out windows drift grows (CKA$\downarrow $ as $n\uparrow $) while forgetting \emph  {falls}: more data moves the encoder further and leaves the model further ahead of zero-shot ($10/10$ seeds negative at $n{=}10{,}000$, Wilson CI $[0.72, 1.00]$). \textbf  {If drift magnitude predicted harm the two columns would rise together; they move in opposite directions.} Scored on the selection windows instead (\emph  {sel.}), forgetting is non-monotonic in $n$, the selection-split effect of \S  \ref {sec:analysis:heldout}. Dispersion here is the sample standard deviation over seeds ($\text  {ddof}{=}1$), not the standard error the rest of the paper reports, because the table is about how the spread across seeds moves with $n$.}}{20}{table.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:sample_sweep}{{5}{20}{\textbf {Sample-size sweep on Moirai-Small/ETTh2} ($h{=}96$, condition~B). On held-out windows drift grows (CKA$\downarrow $ as $n\uparrow $) while forgetting \emph {falls}: more data moves the encoder further and leaves the model further ahead of zero-shot ($10/10$ seeds negative at $n{=}10{,}000$, Wilson CI $[0.72, 1.00]$). \textbf {If drift magnitude predicted harm the two columns would rise together; they move in opposite directions.} Scored on the selection windows instead (\emph {sel.}), forgetting is non-monotonic in $n$, the selection-split effect of \S \ref {sec:analysis:heldout}. Dispersion here is the sample standard deviation over seeds ($\text {ddof}{=}1$), not the standard error the rest of the paper reports, because the table is about how the spread across seeds moves with $n$}{table.caption.12}{}}
