\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces All 31 intervention cells (the 31 of 32 screened cells with a paired B/D run): \textbf  {selection-split} gate $V_{\text  {ridge}}$ (the primary gate of \S  \ref {sec:forecasting:gate}, scored on windows disjoint from the outcome columns beside it), then B$-$D on validation and on held-out windows (positive ${=}$ the frozen encoder was better), and whether the validation and held-out readings disagree in sign. Every row names its own $n$ because $n$ is \emph  {not} constant down the column: the ETTh2 spectrum cells ran at 500 while their same-dataset neighbours ran at 1{,}000, and a row that stayed silent would read as sharing its neighbour's. ILI is the one cell with no cap: it trains on its whole 452-window split, since it is the only series small enough, so its $n$ is a size rather than a subsample. Generated by \texttt  {scripts/cell\_matrix.py --latex}.}}{21}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:heldout_all}{{6}{21}{All 31 intervention cells (the 31 of 32 screened cells with a paired B/D run): \textbf {selection-split} gate $\Vb {\text {ridge}}$ (the primary gate of \S \ref {sec:forecasting:gate}, scored on windows disjoint from the outcome columns beside it), then B$-$D on validation and on held-out windows (positive ${=}$ the frozen encoder was better), and whether the validation and held-out readings disagree in sign. Every row names its own $n$ because $n$ is \emph {not} constant down the column: the ETTh2 spectrum cells ran at 500 while their same-dataset neighbours ran at 1{,}000, and a row that stayed silent would read as sharing its neighbour's. ILI is the one cell with no cap: it trains on its whole 452-window split, since it is the only series small enough, so its $n$ is a size rather than a subsample. Generated by \texttt {scripts/cell\_matrix.py --latex}}{table.caption.14}{}}
