\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {A24}Paired Inference on Encoder Adaptation, and What These Seed Counts Can Resolve}{52}{appendix.X}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:pairedinference}{{A24}{52}{Paired Inference on Encoder Adaptation, and What These Seed Counts Can Resolve}{appendix.X}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {31}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Paired seed-level inference on encoder adaptation.} $\Delta _\text  {enc}={L_\text  {full}}-{L_\text  {frozen}}$ per seed, in percent of the cell's zero-shot reference, so a positive value means allowing the encoder to adapt made the cell \emph  {worse}. B and D share a seed, an initialisation and a window sample, so this contrast is paired by construction. $d$ is paired Cohen's $d$. The SEM column was dropped when $q$ was added (nine columns overrun the textwidth and the interval states the same dispersion), so \texttt  {sem} lives in \texttt  {results/paired\_inference.json}, on the body's convention (\S  \ref {sec:method}). Each row names its own $n$, which is not constant down the column (Table~\ref {tab:heldout_all}). \textbf  {$q$ is the decision column, adjusted for the 31 tests}: Benjamini--Hochberg within this contrast, with the three-way call (freeze-decisive, adapt-decisive, inconclusive) read off $q{<}0.05$ and the sign of $\Delta _\text  {enc}$, and bolded where decisive. BH runs on $p_t$, not $p_\text  {exact}$, because 24 cells have an exact floor of $0.25$ and a step-up procedure there would reject nothing at any effect size. \textbf  {Two tests, because neither alone is adequate at these seed counts.} $p_\text  {exact}$ assumes nothing, but its resolution is fixed by $N$: the 24 cells at $N{=}3$ cannot reach $p<0.05$ at any effect size, their smallest attainable value being $0.25$. $p_t$ and the interval do resolve there, and pay for it with a normality assumption on three points that cannot be checked. Both are shown so that a reading resting on that assumption is visible as one; the text states why no bootstrap interval is reported. In the two summary rows the last column is \emph  {not} an effect size: it gives the number of cells whose mean $\Delta _\text  {enc}$ is positive, out of the cells in that row. $\dagger $ marks cells clearing the gate threshold under at least one admissible baseline of the ladder (Appendix~\ref {app:baselines}). $\ddagger $ marks the Chronos cells, whose per-condition forgetting is confounded by the head/decoder mismatch; their $\Delta _\text  {enc}$ is unaffected.}}{55}{table.caption.44}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:paired_inference}{{31}{55}{\textbf {Paired seed-level inference on encoder adaptation.} $\Delta _\text {enc}={L_\text {full}}-{L_\text {frozen}}$ per seed, in percent of the cell's zero-shot reference, so a positive value means allowing the encoder to adapt made the cell \emph {worse}. B and D share a seed, an initialisation and a window sample, so this contrast is paired by construction. $d$ is paired Cohen's $d$. The SEM column was dropped when $q$ was added (nine columns overrun the textwidth and the interval states the same dispersion), so \texttt {sem} lives in \texttt {results/paired\_inference.json}, on the body's convention (\S \ref {sec:method}). Each row names its own $n$, which is not constant down the column (Table~\ref {tab:heldout_all}). \textbf {$q$ is the decision column, adjusted for the 31 tests}: Benjamini--Hochberg within this contrast, with the three-way call (freeze-decisive, adapt-decisive, inconclusive) read off $q{<}0.05$ and the sign of $\denc $, and bolded where decisive. BH runs on $p_t$, not $p_\text {exact}$, because 24 cells have an exact floor of $0.25$ and a step-up procedure there would reject nothing at any effect size. \textbf {Two tests, because neither alone is adequate at these seed counts.} $p_\text {exact}$ assumes nothing, but its resolution is fixed by $N$: the 24 cells at $N{=}3$ cannot reach $p<0.05$ at any effect size, their smallest attainable value being $0.25$. $p_t$ and the interval do resolve there, and pay for it with a normality assumption on three points that cannot be checked. Both are shown so that a reading resting on that assumption is visible as one; the text states why no bootstrap interval is reported. In the two summary rows the last column is \emph {not} an effect size: it gives the number of cells whose mean $\Delta _\text {enc}$ is positive, out of the cells in that row. $\dagger $ marks cells clearing the gate threshold under at least one admissible baseline of the ladder (Appendix~\ref {app:baselines}). $\ddagger $ marks the Chronos cells, whose per-condition forgetting is confounded by the head/decoder mismatch; their $\Delta _\text {enc}$ is unaffected}{table.caption.44}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {32}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What each surviving cell's own seed budget could detect.} The 7 cells clearing $V_{\text  {ridge}}{\geq }0.20$, with the minimum detectable effect implied by their own dispersion: MDE $= t_{1-\alpha /2,n-1}\,\text  {sd}/\sqrt  {n}$, the smallest $|\Delta _\text  {enc}|$ a two-sided paired $t$ would resolve at that $n$. $n^\star $ is the paired seed count at which the \emph  {observed} effect would resolve, holding the observed sd fixed; it is therefore a post-hoc estimate and not a design calculation. \textbf  {Two columns, because a single test and a decisive call cost different budgets.} $n^\star $ is computed at an uncorrected $\alpha {=}0.05$; $n^\star _\text  {adj}$ is computed at $\alpha /m$ with $m{=}31$ tests, which bounds the Benjamini--Hochberg threshold the call in Table~\ref {tab:paired_inference} actually uses from below and so bounds the budget from above. Reporting only $n^\star $ would say Moirai-Base/ETTh2 $h{=}192$ is already resolved at $n{=}3$, which its own $q{=}0.018$ happens to agree with, and would say the same of $h{=}96$, whose $q$ is $0.071$. \textbf  {The 4 starred rows are the point of the table}: they need more than our feasibility cap of 20 paired seeds ($\approx $20 GPU-hours per cell at two fine-tunes per seed), so we report them as \emph  {unresolvable under this design}, with the number, rather than as effects that are absent.}}{56}{table.caption.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:power_mde}{{32}{56}{\textbf {What each surviving cell's own seed budget could detect.} The 7 cells clearing $\Vb {\text {ridge}}{\geq }0.20$, with the minimum detectable effect implied by their own dispersion: MDE $= t_{1-\alpha /2,n-1}\,\text {sd}/\sqrt {n}$, the smallest $|\denc |$ a two-sided paired $t$ would resolve at that $n$. $n^\star $ is the paired seed count at which the \emph {observed} effect would resolve, holding the observed sd fixed; it is therefore a post-hoc estimate and not a design calculation. \textbf {Two columns, because a single test and a decisive call cost different budgets.} $n^\star $ is computed at an uncorrected $\alpha {=}0.05$; $n^\star _\text {adj}$ is computed at $\alpha /m$ with $m{=}31$ tests, which bounds the Benjamini--Hochberg threshold the call in Table~\ref {tab:paired_inference} actually uses from below and so bounds the budget from above. Reporting only $n^\star $ would say Moirai-Base/ETTh2 $h{=}192$ is already resolved at $n{=}3$, which its own $q{=}0.018$ happens to agree with, and would say the same of $h{=}96$, whose $q$ is $0.071$. \textbf {The 4 starred rows are the point of the table}: they need more than our feasibility cap of 20 paired seeds ($\approx $20 GPU-hours per cell at two fine-tunes per seed), so we report them as \emph {unresolvable under this design}, with the number, rather than as effects that are absent}{table.caption.45}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {33}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The pre-registered seed top-up, beside the published seed counts.} The 7 cells clearing $V_{\text  {ridge}}{\geq }0.20$. 3 were topped up under \texttt  {results/power\_topup/preregistration\_power.json}, which fixed which cells, which seeds and how many (26 runs) before any of those runs existed; all 26 completed. \textbf  {No published number is replaced.} Each cell's \emph  {published} row is the analysis of Table~\ref {tab:paired_inference}, re-verified against \texttt  {results/paired\_inference.json} at emit time; its \emph  {augmented} row is a second computation with the added seeds admitted, on the same estimator and the same Benjamini--Hochberg correction over the same 31 cells. \textbf  {One call changes}: Moirai-S / ETTm2 ($h{=}192$, $n{=}$1000) moves from inconclusive to freezing-decisive, first at $n{=}12$, with the rung below it at $q{=}0.059$. Across all 31 cells the augmented read changes that call and no other (published 2 freezing-better, 4 adaptation-better, 25 inconclusive; augmented 3, 4, 24). The 2 cells on the \emph  {uncorrected} tier were run to a budget below their BH-adjusted requirement and were registered as \emph  {expected to remain inconclusive}, which held for 1 of the 2 (Moirai-S / ETTh2 ($h{=}192$, $n{=}$500)) and failed for Moirai-S / ETTm2 ($h{=}192$, $n{=}$1000). A failed registered prediction is reported as one; the registration is not edited. The ladder behind ``first at'' admits each cell's added seeds in registered order with the other topped-up cells at their final $n$, so a change is attributable to that cell's own seeds and not to BH moving underneath it. The last column gives each cell's registered budget and the tier it came from (adj.\ = the BH-adjusted requirement, unc.\ = the uncorrected one), or the reason it was not topped up.}}{56}{table.caption.46}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:power_topup}{{33}{56}{\textbf {The pre-registered seed top-up, beside the published seed counts.} The 7 cells clearing $\Vb {\text {ridge}}{\geq }0.20$. 3 were topped up under \texttt {results/power\_topup/preregistration\_power.json}, which fixed which cells, which seeds and how many (26 runs) before any of those runs existed; all 26 completed. \textbf {No published number is replaced.} Each cell's \emph {published} row is the analysis of Table~\ref {tab:paired_inference}, re-verified against \texttt {results/paired\_inference.json} at emit time; its \emph {augmented} row is a second computation with the added seeds admitted, on the same estimator and the same Benjamini--Hochberg correction over the same 31 cells. \textbf {One call changes}: Moirai-S / ETTm2 ($h{=}192$, $n{=}$1000) moves from inconclusive to freezing-decisive, first at $n{=}12$, with the rung below it at $q{=}0.059$. Across all 31 cells the augmented read changes that call and no other (published 2 freezing-better, 4 adaptation-better, 25 inconclusive; augmented 3, 4, 24). The 2 cells on the \emph {uncorrected} tier were run to a budget below their BH-adjusted requirement and were registered as \emph {expected to remain inconclusive}, which held for 1 of the 2 (Moirai-S / ETTh2 ($h{=}192$, $n{=}$500)) and failed for Moirai-S / ETTm2 ($h{=}192$, $n{=}$1000). A failed registered prediction is reported as one; the registration is not edited. The ladder behind ``first at'' admits each cell's added seeds in registered order with the other topped-up cells at their final $n$, so a change is attributable to that cell's own seeds and not to BH moving underneath it. The last column gives each cell's registered budget and the tier it came from (adj.\ = the BH-adjusted requirement, unc.\ = the uncorrected one), or the reason it was not topped up}{table.caption.46}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {A25}The Graded Value Axis: Is the Dissociation Confined to Cells With Nothing to Lose?}{57}{appendix.Y}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:valueaxis}{{A25}{57}{The Graded Value Axis: Is the Dissociation Confined to Cells With Nothing to Lose?}{appendix.Y}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Neither drift nor measured pre-trained value orders the encoder intervention.} Both panels share $y{=}$ held-out $\Delta _\text  {enc}$ (positive $=$ freezing the encoder did better). Point area is proportional to the graded value score in \emph  {both} panels, so the left panel answers the objection visually: if drift predicted the outcome wherever there was something to lose, the large points would trend and the small ones would scatter. Backbone is encoded by colour \emph  {and} marker because CKA is not comparable across architectures, which is why the pooled left-panel interval must not be read as a within-backbone one. Drawn by \texttt  {paper\_8/fig\_value\_axis.py} from \texttt  {results/value\_axis.json}; the script asserts its own counts against that file and fails rather than drawing a figure that disagrees with this text.}}{57}{figure.caption.47}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:valueaxis}{{6}{57}{\textbf {Neither drift nor measured pre-trained value orders the encoder intervention.} Both panels share $y{=}$ held-out $\denc $ (positive $=$ freezing the encoder did better). Point area is proportional to the graded value score in \emph {both} panels, so the left panel answers the objection visually: if drift predicted the outcome wherever there was something to lose, the large points would trend and the small ones would scatter. Backbone is encoded by colour \emph {and} marker because CKA is not comparable across architectures, which is why the pooled left-panel interval must not be read as a within-backbone one. Drawn by \texttt {paper\_8/fig\_value\_axis.py} from \texttt {results/value\_axis.json}; the script asserts its own counts against that file and fails rather than drawing a figure that disagrees with this text}{figure.caption.47}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {34}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The dependence, estimated two ways.} Rank rows are Spearman $\rho $ with $10{,}000$ bootstrap draws over $(\text  {backbone}, \text  {dataset})$ clusters, seed~$0$. The slope rows fit all cells at once with backbone identity absorbed as a fixed effect and both regressors centred, so $b_1$ is the CKA slope \emph  {at mean value}; $b_3$ is the quantity at issue. Emitted by \texttt  {scripts/value\_axis.py --latex}, which delegates the slope fit to \texttt  {scripts/cka\_fixed\_effects.py} rather than refitting it.}}{58}{table.caption.48}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:valueaxis}{{34}{58}{\textbf {The dependence, estimated two ways.} Rank rows are Spearman $\rho $ with $10{,}000$ bootstrap draws over $(\text {backbone}, \text {dataset})$ clusters, seed~$0$. The slope rows fit all cells at once with backbone identity absorbed as a fixed effect and both regressors centred, so $b_1$ is the CKA slope \emph {at mean value}; $b_3$ is the quantity at issue. Emitted by \texttt {scripts/value\_axis.py --latex}, which delegates the slope fit to \texttt {scripts/cka\_fixed\_effects.py} rather than refitting it}{table.caption.48}{}}
