\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces Within-backbone slope of held-out B$-$D on CKA, in percentage points per unit of CKA, with backbone identity absorbed as a fixed effect rather than by subsetting. Intervals are $10{,}000$ cluster-bootstrap draws over the 15 $(\text  {backbone}, \text  {dataset})$ clusters of 31 cells; draws in which the fixed effects are unidentified or the design is rank-deficient are discarded, and the kept count is reported. Emitted by \texttt  {scripts/clustered\_inference.py} from \texttt  {scripts/cka\_fixed\_effects.py}.}}{64}{table.caption.53}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:clustered_fe}{{38}{64}{Within-backbone slope of held-out B$-$D on CKA, in percentage points per unit of CKA, with backbone identity absorbed as a fixed effect rather than by subsetting. Intervals are $10{,}000$ cluster-bootstrap draws over the 15 $(\text {backbone}, \text {dataset})$ clusters of 31 cells; draws in which the fixed effects are unidentified or the design is rank-deficient are discarded, and the kept count is reported. Emitted by \texttt {scripts/clustered\_inference.py} from \texttt {scripts/cka\_fixed\_effects.py}}{table.caption.53}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {A30}Does CKA Add Predictive Information Beyond Backbone Identity? A Pre-Specified Out-of-Sample Test}{65}{appendix.Alph30}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:loco}{{A30}{65}{Does CKA Add Predictive Information Beyond Backbone Identity? A Pre-Specified Out-of-Sample Test}{appendix.Alph30}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {A30.1}The Same Cells Priced as a Decision, Post-Hoc}{66}{subsection.Alph30.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{app:decision}{{A30.1}{66}{The Same Cells Priced as a Decision, Post-Hoc}{subsection.Alph30.1}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {39}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Does CKA add out-of-sample predictive information about $\Delta _\text  {enc}$ beyond backbone identity?} Nested models over all 31 scored cells, validated by leave-one-cluster-out across the 15 (backbone, dataset) clusters. $R^2_\text  {LOCO}$ is scored \textbf  {against the training-fold mean}, never the held-out fold's own mean, so a positive value means the model beats predicting the other clusters' average; MAE is in percentage points of zero-shot loss. \textbf  {The deciding statistic and this table's interpretation were fixed before the models were fitted} (\texttt  {results/preregister\_loco.json}): $\Delta R^2 = R^2_\text  {LOCO}(\text  {M3}) - R^2_\text  {LOCO}(\text  {M2}) = -0.025$, median bootstrap draw $-0.083$, IQR $[-0.227, +0.043]$, and adding CKA fails to help in 69\% of draws; $\Delta \text  {MAE} = +0.59$~pp, IQR $[-0.37, +2.98]$. \textbf  {Interquartile ranges, not 95\% intervals, are quoted for both}: an out-of-sample $R^2$ is unbounded below and a degenerate bootstrap fold can blow up either statistic, so their extreme percentiles are not reproducible: $\Delta R^2$'s 2.5th percentile moved between $-1.7$ and $-32.5$ across bootstrap seeds, and $\Delta $MAE's 97.5th between $+11$ and $+19$, while the point estimates, medians and sign share moved in the third decimal. \textbf  {The 95\% interval is carried instead by the one linear coefficient here}, CKA's slope in M3: $+43$~pp per unit CKA, cluster-bootstrap CI $[-31, +107]$, which covers zero, so the slope's sign is not even determined, let alone its size. That interval is quoted to whole percentage points because its bounds move by under 5~pp across bootstrap seeds. The pre-registered deciding statistic (the \emph  {sign} of $\Delta R^2$) is unchanged; only the dispersion measure beside it differs from the registration, which says so. Dataset identity is deliberately \emph  {absent} from the ladder: a cluster is a (backbone, dataset) pair, so holding one out removes the only cells that identify that dataset's fixed effect and the fold would silently score a model nobody specified: three of the 7 series are covered by a single backbone, so no other fold could identify them even in principle. The in-sample fit \emph  {with} dataset fixed effects is reported in the text and is not validation.}}{67}{table.caption.54}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cka_loco}{{39}{67}{\textbf {Does CKA add out-of-sample predictive information about $\denc $ beyond backbone identity?} Nested models over all 31 scored cells, validated by leave-one-cluster-out across the 15 (backbone, dataset) clusters. $R^2_\text {LOCO}$ is scored \textbf {against the training-fold mean}, never the held-out fold's own mean, so a positive value means the model beats predicting the other clusters' average; MAE is in percentage points of zero-shot loss. \textbf {The deciding statistic and this table's interpretation were fixed before the models were fitted} (\texttt {results/preregister\_loco.json}): $\Delta R^2 = R^2_\text {LOCO}(\text {M3}) - R^2_\text {LOCO}(\text {M2}) = -0.025$, median bootstrap draw $-0.083$, IQR $[-0.227, +0.043]$, and adding CKA fails to help in 69\% of draws; $\Delta \text {MAE} = +0.59$~pp, IQR $[-0.37, +2.98]$. \textbf {Interquartile ranges, not 95\% intervals, are quoted for both}: an out-of-sample $R^2$ is unbounded below and a degenerate bootstrap fold can blow up either statistic, so their extreme percentiles are not reproducible: $\Delta R^2$'s 2.5th percentile moved between $-1.7$ and $-32.5$ across bootstrap seeds, and $\Delta $MAE's 97.5th between $+11$ and $+19$, while the point estimates, medians and sign share moved in the third decimal. \textbf {The 95\% interval is carried instead by the one linear coefficient here}, CKA's slope in M3: $+43$~pp per unit CKA, cluster-bootstrap CI $[-31, +107]$, which covers zero, so the slope's sign is not even determined, let alone its size. That interval is quoted to whole percentage points because its bounds move by under 5~pp across bootstrap seeds. The pre-registered deciding statistic (the \emph {sign} of $\Delta R^2$) is unchanged; only the dispersion measure beside it differs from the registration, which says so. Dataset identity is deliberately \emph {absent} from the ladder: a cluster is a (backbone, dataset) pair, so holding one out removes the only cells that identify that dataset's fixed effect and the fold would silently score a model nobody specified: three of the 7 series are covered by a single backbone, so no other fold could identify them even in principle. The in-sample fit \emph {with} dataset fixed effects is reported in the text and is not validation}{table.caption.54}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {40}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {What would acting on CKA have saved?} The same 31 cells read as a decision rather than a correlation. A practitioner choosing per cell between full fine-tuning and a frozen encoder collects $\Delta _\text  {enc}$ (positive ${=}$ freezing better) on every cell they freeze, so \emph  {saving} $=$ mean over all 31 cells of $\Delta _\text  {enc}\cdot \mathbf  {1}[\text  {policy freezes}]$, in percentage points of MSE, against the default of always adapting. \textbf  {The out-of-sample row is the only one a practitioner could have followed}: the threshold and its orientation are searched on the 14 training clusters and applied unchanged to the held-out cluster, so each cell's decision is made without seeing it. The row below it is the same search run on all cells at once, and the difference between them, $+1.10$~pp $[0, +13]$, prices the optimism in a retrospectively chosen cut. \textbf  {The headline is the interval, not the point estimate}: the best-orientation rule's $+7.47$~pp comes with $[-4, +16]$ and is ${\leq }0$ in 25\% of draws, so it is a policy whose value these cells cannot establish, not one they refute. \textbf  {And the orientation that earns it is the inverse of the prescribed one}: freezing where drift is \emph  {largest} -- what forgetting mitigation actually recommends -- is worth $-6.97$~pp $[-10, +14]$, and the row above it buys its point estimate by freezing the cells that moved \emph  {least}, a direction available only because we searched both. That rule still puts 8 of the 31 cells on the wrong side of its own decision out of sample (16 of them wanted freezing), against 5 for the in-sample cut. \textbf  {This analysis is post-hoc and descriptive}: unlike Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco}, nothing here was pre-registered, and the family searched is a single global CKA threshold in either orientation -- a per-backbone or multivariate rule could do better, and 15 clusters cannot rule that out. Candidate cuts are interior (midpoints between consecutive distinct CKA values), so the search cannot return \emph  {always freeze} or \emph  {always adapt} and have it counted as CKA informing the decision; both are priced as rows of their own. Intervals are 95\% percentile cluster bootstrap (10,000/10,000 draws kept, a draw discarded when it leaves fewer than three distinct clusters), re-running the whole leave-one-cluster-out procedure inside each draw with duplicated clusters sharing one fold id. Every statistic is a mean of bounded per-cell quantities, so unlike $\Delta R^2$ in Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco} these percentiles are reproducible -- \textbf  {to whole percentage points, which is why they are quoted that way}: at two decimals the bounds move across bootstrap seeds (this table's headline lower bound came out $-3.97$, $-3.61$, $-3.68$ and $-3.67$ on seeds 0, 1, 7 and 13), and \texttt  {-{}-seedcheck} checks every figure quoted here at its printed precision. The point estimates and the misplacement counts carry no seed at all.}}{68}{table.caption.55}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cka_decision}{{40}{68}{\textbf {What would acting on CKA have saved?} The same 31 cells read as a decision rather than a correlation. A practitioner choosing per cell between full fine-tuning and a frozen encoder collects $\denc $ (positive ${=}$ freezing better) on every cell they freeze, so \emph {saving} $=$ mean over all 31 cells of $\denc \cdot \mathbf {1}[\text {policy freezes}]$, in percentage points of MSE, against the default of always adapting. \textbf {The out-of-sample row is the only one a practitioner could have followed}: the threshold and its orientation are searched on the 14 training clusters and applied unchanged to the held-out cluster, so each cell's decision is made without seeing it. The row below it is the same search run on all cells at once, and the difference between them, $+1.10$~pp $[0, +13]$, prices the optimism in a retrospectively chosen cut. \textbf {The headline is the interval, not the point estimate}: the best-orientation rule's $+7.47$~pp comes with $[-4, +16]$ and is ${\leq }0$ in 25\% of draws, so it is a policy whose value these cells cannot establish, not one they refute. \textbf {And the orientation that earns it is the inverse of the prescribed one}: freezing where drift is \emph {largest} -- what forgetting mitigation actually recommends -- is worth $-6.97$~pp $[-10, +14]$, and the row above it buys its point estimate by freezing the cells that moved \emph {least}, a direction available only because we searched both. That rule still puts 8 of the 31 cells on the wrong side of its own decision out of sample (16 of them wanted freezing), against 5 for the in-sample cut. \textbf {This analysis is post-hoc and descriptive}: unlike Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco}, nothing here was pre-registered, and the family searched is a single global CKA threshold in either orientation -- a per-backbone or multivariate rule could do better, and 15 clusters cannot rule that out. Candidate cuts are interior (midpoints between consecutive distinct CKA values), so the search cannot return \emph {always freeze} or \emph {always adapt} and have it counted as CKA informing the decision; both are priced as rows of their own. Intervals are 95\% percentile cluster bootstrap (10,000/10,000 draws kept, a draw discarded when it leaves fewer than three distinct clusters), re-running the whole leave-one-cluster-out procedure inside each draw with duplicated clusters sharing one fold id. Every statistic is a mean of bounded per-cell quantities, so unlike $\Delta R^2$ in Table~\ref {tab:cka_loco} these percentiles are reproducible -- \textbf {to whole percentage points, which is why they are quoted that way}: at two decimals the bounds move across bootstrap seeds (this table's headline lower bound came out $-3.97$, $-3.61$, $-3.68$ and $-3.67$ on seeds 0, 1, 7 and 13), and \texttt {-{}-seedcheck} checks every figure quoted here at its printed precision. The point estimates and the misplacement counts carry no seed at all}{table.caption.55}{}}
